- What metrics would you collect to detect strategy performance regressions?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your understanding of runtime extensibility, concurrency safety, and clean separation between core rendering logic and plugin points.

## Key Concepts
- **Runtime plugin registry**: A centralized map that stores helper factories, enabling registration without engine rebuilds
- **Template compilation**: Templates are parsed into an intermediate representation that makes runtime calls to resolved helpers
- **Scope isolation**: Distinguish between global helpers (available to all templates) and per-template helpers (isolated to specific template instances)
- **Error handling**: Graceful degradation when helpers are missing, with clear error messages and optional fallback behavior
- **Thread safety**: Considerations for concurrent access in multi-threaded environments

## Code Example
```typescript
type HelperFn = (arg: string, ctx: Record<string, unknown>) => string;
type HelperFactory = () => HelperFn;

class HelperRegistry {
  private store = new Map<string, HelperFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: HelperFactory): void {
    this.store.set(name, factory);
  }

  resolve(name: string): HelperFn {
    const factory = this.store.get(name);
    if (!factory) {
      throw new Error(`Helper '${name}' not found`);
    }
    return factory();
  }

  has(name: string): boolean {
    return this.store.has(name);
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new HelperRegistry();

registry.register('uppercase', () => (s) => s.toUpperCase());
registry.register('dateFormat', () => (s, ctx) => 
  new Date(s).toLocaleDateString(ctx.locale ?? 'en-US'));
registry.register('currency', () => (s, ctx) => 
  Number(s).toLocaleString(ctx.currency ?? 'USD', { style: 'currency', currency: ctx.currency ?? 'USD' }));

// Render example
function render(template: string, ctx: Record<string, unknown>, registry: HelperRegistry): string {
  return template.replace(/\{\{(\w+)\s+(.+?)\}\}/g, (_, helper, arg) => {
    try {
      return registry.resolve(helper)(arg, ctx);
    } catch (e) {
      return `[Error: ${(e as Error).message}]`;
    }
  });
}

// Example: {{uppercase hello}}, {{dateFormat 2024-01-15}}, {{currency 99.99}}
```

## Scope Considerations
- **Global helpers**: Registered once on the shared registry; all templates can access them. Use when helpers are universally applicable.
- **Per-template helpers**: Passed at render time or scoped to specific template instances. Use when helpers need isolation or template-specific behavior.

## Error Handling
- Throw descriptive errors for missing helpers
- Provide a `has()` method for optional fallback logic
- Consider wrapping render errors to continue processing other placeholders

## Thread Safety
- Use immutable snapshots or copy-on-write for concurrent reads
- Employ mutexes (e.g., `ReadWriteLock`) for registration during runtime
- In single-threaded JS environments, the registry is naturally safe; focus on clean async handling for worker threads

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to extend system behavior at runtime, wire loosely coupled components, and reason about composition and short-circuiting in a production-grade policy engine.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy: encapsulate each policy as a separate evaluation strategy.
- Registry: dynamic registration and lookup of policies by name.
- Composition: PolicySet aggregates multiple policies and enforces all to pass (short-circuit on deny).
- Isolation: policies run independently to avoid cascading failures.

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Context { user: string; resource: string; action: string; time: Date }
interface Policy { name: string; evaluate(ctx: Context): boolean }
class PolicyRegistry { private map = new Map<string, Policy>(); register(p: Policy){ this.map.set(p.name, p); } get(name: string){ return this.map.get(name); } }
class PolicySet { constructor(private registry: PolicyRegistry, private active: string[] = []) {} addPolicy(name: string){ this.active.push(name); } evaluate(ctx: Context): boolean { for (const name of this.active){ const p = this.registry.get(name); if (p && !p.evaluate(ctx)) return false; } return true } }
class RBACPolicy implements Policy { name = 'rbac'; evaluate(ctx: Context){ return ctx.user === 'admin' || (ctx.user === 'user' && ctx.action === 'read'); } }
class TimePolicy implements Policy { name = 'time'; evaluate(ctx: Context){ const hour = ctx.time.getUTCHours(); return hour >= 9 && hour <= 17; } }

const reg = new PolicyRegistry(); reg.register(new RBACPolicy()); reg.register(new TimePolicy());
const ps = new PolicySet(reg, ['rbac','time']);
const ok = ps.evaluate({ user:'user', resource:'doc', action:'read', time: new Date()});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support asynchronous policy checks (external lookups) in the same framework?
- How would you handle policy conflicts and define prioritization or weighting between policies?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses your ability to design an extensible data pipeline architecture that remains stable as new formats are added, using registries and factories to achieve loose coupling.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy pattern for pluggable parser implementations
- Registry pattern to map file extensions to appropriate parsers
- Factory pattern to instantiate parsers at runtime
- Interface segregation with minimal, focused contracts

## Code Example

```javascript
interface Parser {
  parse(data: string): Record<string, any>[];
}

interface ParserFactory {
  create(): Parser;
}

class ParserRegistry {
  constructor() {
    this.parsers = new Map();
  }

  register(extension, factory) {
    this.parsers.set(extension, factory);
  }

  getParser(extension) {
    const factory = this.parsers.get(extension);
    return factory ? factory.create() : null;
  }
}

class Importer {
  constructor(registry) {
    this.registry = registry;
  }

  async import(filePath, data) {
    const extension = filePath.split('.').pop();
    const parser = this.registry.getParser(extension);

    if (!parser) {
      throw new Error(`No parser found for .${extension} files`);
    }

    return parser.parse(data);
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new ParserRegistry();
registry.register('csv', () => new CsvParser());
registry.register('json', () => new JsonParser());
registry.register('tsv', () => new TsvParser());

const importer = new Importer(registry);
```

## Implementation Benefits

- **Extensibility**: New formats added without modifying core importer
- **Decoupling**: Registry isolates format-specific logic
- **Type Safety**: Interfaces ensure consistent parser contracts
- **Runtime Flexibility**: Parsers instantiated based on file extension

## Why This Is Asked
Probes runtime extensibility for logging paths and sampling to keep core stable while adding sinks/strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Plugin Registry pattern
- Strategy pattern for sampling
- Open/Closed principles and hot-swapping
- Thread-safety and lock-free reads
- Structured logging interfaces

## Code Example
```typescript
type LogEvent = { timestamp: number; level: string; message: string; meta?: any };
export interface ISink { init(): void; write(event: LogEvent): void; }
export interface Sampler { shouldSample(event: LogEvent, context?: any): boolean; }
export class SinkRegistry { private map = new Map<string, ISink>(); register(n: string, s: ISink){ this.map.set(n, s); } get(n: string){ return this.map.get(n)!; } }
export class SamplerRegistry { private map = new Map<string, Sampler>(); register(n: string, s: Sampler){ this.map.set(n, s); } get(n: string){ return this.map.get(n)!; } }
export class CoreLogger {
  constructor(private sinks: SinkRegistry, private samplers: SamplerRegistry, private defaultSink: string, private defaultSampler: string) {}
  log(event: LogEvent, sinkName?: string, samplerName?: string){
    const s = sinkName ?? this.defaultSink; const sp = samplerName ?? this.defaultSampler;
    if (this.samplers.get(sp).shouldSample(event)) {
      this.sinks.get(s).write(event);
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to enforce lifecycle of sinks (init/close) and handle failures?
- How many sinks can be hot-swapped safely during traffic spikes?
- How to test sampling correctness with time-based workloads?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design extensible text processing with runtime plugins, without touching core; demonstrates a clean separation of concerns and Open/Closed compliance.

## Key Concepts
- Pipeline/Chain of Responsibility pattern
- Runtime registry for extensibility
- Minimal transform interface and composition order
- Deterministic, idempotent transforms

## Code Example

```javascript
class Transform {
  constructor(name, fn) { this.name = name; this.fn = fn; }
  apply(text) { return this.fn(text); }
}
class Registry {
  constructor(){ this.factories = new Map(); }
  register(name, factory){ this.factories.set(name, factory); }
  create(name){ const f = this.factories.get(name); return f ? f() : null; }
}
class Pipeline {
  constructor(registry, names){ this.transforms = names.map(n => registry.create(n)).filter(t => t); }
  run(input){ return this.transforms.reduce((acc, t) => t.apply(acc), input); }
}

const registry = new Registry();
registry.register('trim', () => new Transform('trim', s => s.trim()));
registry.register('collapseSpaces', () => new Transform('collapseSpaces', s => s.replace(/\s+/g, ' ')));
registry.register('toUpper', () => new Transform('toUpper', s => s.toUpperCase()));

const names = ['trim','collapseSpaces','toUpper'];
const pipeline = new Pipeline(registry, names);
console.log(pipeline.run('  hello   world  ')); // 'HELLO WORLD'
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle asynchronous transforms in this pipeline?
- How would you order or deduplicate transforms registered at runtime to guarantee deterministic results?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of a small, runtime-extendable data pipeline using common patterns. It’s approachable for beginners and maps to real-world data processing tasks.

## Key Concepts

- Registry pattern
- Pipeline/Chain execution
- Runtime extensibility and isolation
- Error handling and testing

## Code Example

```javascript
interface Transformer { transform(input: any): any; }
class Registry {
  private map = new Map<string, () => Transformer>();
  register(name: string, factory: () => Transformer) { this.map.set(name, factory); }
  create(name: string): Transformer | undefined { const f = this.map.get(name); return f ? f() : undefined; }
}
class Pipeline {
  constructor(private registry: Registry, private steps: string[]) {}
  run(input: any): any {
    let data = input;
    for (const name of this.steps) {
      const t = this.registry.create(name);
      if (!t) throw new Error(`Unknown transformer: ${name}`);
      data = t.transform(data);
    }
    return data;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you support asynchronous transformers?
- How would you unit-test pipelines and ensure isolation between transformers?
